WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.853
Scorn is a game designed to frighten you.

00:00:08.853 --> 00:00:12.321
It depicts a biomechanical nightmare

00:00:12.321 --> 00:00:19.050
a fever dream where everything you see is a
shocking amalgamation of metal and meat. Yet

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:25.539
under the foreboding surface, the world of
Scorn is a masterpiece of haunting and poignant

00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:31.710
worldbuilding, bringing to life a fascinating
ecosystem of flesh and decay. While the game

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:38.110
is ambiguous by design — lacking any text
or dialogue — a surprising number of answers

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:43.899
are waiting for those willing to look for
them. So, for this entry into the Archive,

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:50.039
we’ll embark on an expedition across this
disturbing realm, comparing environmental

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:56.110
details with clues from the artbook to uncover
the shocking secrets behind the terrors on

00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:06.950
screen. Now, let’s awaken to the nightmare
of Scorn… We’ll begin with a birth. The

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:12.990
humanoid creatures of the Scorn universe aren’t
born from a nurturing parent, but burst fully

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:18.640
grown from an unnatural wall, and then plummet
to the ground, where many of their stories

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:25.140
end as abruptly as they began. The humanoid
protagonist you play as for most of the game

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:31.210
comes into the world from this unfeeling system.
After hitting the ground, it’s hard not

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:36.750
to stare up at the rows and rows of ruptured
cocoons and think about what it would be like

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:43.009
if this was the first thing you ever saw.
This immense structure, referred to in the

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:49.450
artbook as the Genesis Wall, is one of the
most striking concepts in the world of Scorn.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:55.380
Dried husks lie beneath the embankment — humanoids
who perished through no fault of their own.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:01.329
There’s a profound unfairness at play that
echoes the arbitrary nature of life, one of

00:02:01.329 --> 00:02:07.790
the game’s core themes. Like many things
in Scorn, the wall seems both artificial and

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:13.430
biological, as there are places where the
outer layer falls away to reveal a mass of

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:20.250
living organs. While the design of the Genesis
Wall was conceived through stunning concept

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:27.480
art, where the wall came from is one question
the game’s developer, Ebb Software, declines

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:38.099
to answer. The wall simply is — a fact as
immutable as existence itself. Beyond the

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:43.670
wall’s shadow, a desolate wasteland greets
newborn humanoids as they take their first

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:50.700
steps. Called ‘The Field,’ this is a kingdom
of dust and ruin — a barren land seemingly

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:57.190
stripped of all natural resources. Through
the fog, the forms of distant buildings reveal

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:03.730
themselves — wind-worn structures silently
decomposing like the carcasses of gargantuan

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:10.750
animals. These haunting vistas conjure the
imagery of decaying ships present in sci-fi

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:17.319
horror films like Alien and Prometheus. The
late Swiss artist H. R. Giger, who worked

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:23.470
on the biomechanical designs for the original
Alien film, was a notable influence on the

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:29.959
world of Scorn. Some of the decaying ruins
form vaguely organic archways, which serve

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:36.400
as a subtle way to pull players onwards. One
of these thresholds leads to a cemetery of

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:43.650
machines, where industrial vents mark the
landscape like solemn gravestones. At a glance,

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:49.959
this industrial mausoleum seems as barren
as the rest of The Field. Yet defying all

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:56.040
odds, a strange form of life takes shelter
among the headstones. Seemingly originating

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:01.379
all the way back at the Genesis Wall, red
tendrils spread across the wastes like an

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:07.180
insidious infection, and like hermit crabs
seeking a shell, some masses of tentacles

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:13.349
have come to make their home within the vents.
These parasitic grows are all that can survive

00:04:13.349 --> 00:04:22.280
this unforgiving climate. To the side of the
cemetery, the largest building in the region

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:29.370
is a monolithic structure called The Assembly.
The last bastion of shelter in a ruined land,

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:34.930
many forms of life make their home within.
Entering The Assembly is like exploring the

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:41.430
body of a long-dead giant. The hallways and
cavernous chambers call to mind fossilized

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:48.250
organs, with the ribbed pattern on the walls
unmistakably organic. This biomechanical interior

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:55.280
design once again evokes the spirit of Giger’s
work in the Alien franchise, where even buildings

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.500
take on the qualities of living things. While
the bodily systems of The Assembly have long

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.970
lain dormant, you can guide the playable humanoid
to reawaken the decrepit technology. But be

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:14.170
aware: Interacting with technology in Scorn
is never pleasant. Buttons and switches are

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:20.500
fleshy and bizarre, to the point where something
as simple as calling an elevator is vaguely

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:26.750
unsettling. And the more you explore and interact
with the interior, the more the original purpose

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:33.130
of The Assembly becomes apparent. The entire
labyrinthine structure functions as a kind

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:40.230
of factory. Enormous retro-industrial cranes
are positioned to move cargo, their articulated

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:46.630
hooks still sharp enough to be menacing. Spine-like
railways snake through The Assembly, implying

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:53.010
transport carts once rattled through this
area. An elevated control panel provides access

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:59.790
to a manipulatory claw-arm — like the world’s
largest and most disturbing arcade machine.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:06.570
Yet a question remains: just what was this
factory processing? The answer is the last

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:14.960
available resource in the world of Scorn:
living things. At the base of this world’s

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:22.250
biomechanical ecosystem are the tragic Moldmen.
These pitiable lifeforms are the biomass The

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:28.699
Assembly is designed to produce, and are grown
within hard shells. Heaps of Moldmen husks

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:34.090
have been swept away into the corners of the
Assembly, with the size of these piles giving

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:40.160
us some glimpse of the scale of this factory’s
inhumanity when it was in full production.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.960
The visuals of the Moldmen are evocative of
the work of Polish artist Zdzisław Beksiński,

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:53.199
a painter who explored themes of entropy and
decay. Beksiński’s art was a key influence

00:06:53.199 --> 00:07:00.070
on the look of the game, with its emphasis
on somber visions of dried, calcified husks.

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:05.669
The imagery of a Moldman sitting in a transport
chair with their limbs extending out from

00:07:05.669 --> 00:07:12.710
the shell also calls to mind the famous Giger
portrait of the Space Jockey. This ambiguous

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:18.979
visual of a being seemingly fused with a chair,
which was brought to life in the Alien franchise,

00:07:18.979 --> 00:07:25.400
was a key influence in Scorn’s development.
According to concept artist Filip Acovic,

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:31.210
the team found inspiration not just in how
the design merged technology and living tissue,

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:37.789
but also in its mysterious nature. In the
original Alien film, next to nothing is revealed

00:07:37.789 --> 00:07:44.460
about the strange being, and that uncertainty
encourages the viewer to reflect on the visual.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.379
Likewise, much about the Moldmen and their
connection to The Assembly’s creators is

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:57.840
left an intriguing mystery. Deeper within
The Assembly, monolithic towers of tissue

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:04.530
grow in temperature-controlled, greenhouse-like
chambers. Like so many things in Scorn, the

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:10.041
purpose of these looming structures is left
ambiguous, with the mystery gnawing at the

00:08:10.041 --> 00:08:15.820
back of the players’ mind as they walk in
their shadow. A melting pattern of what appears

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:22.280
to be calcified bone encircles the tops of
these columns, suggesting they produce organic

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:28.430
material used elsewhere in the factory. A
legion of flying drones tends to the tissue

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:34.839
towers’ every need, hydrating the chamber
like devoted gardeners. Each drone can release

00:08:34.839 --> 00:08:40.580
a steady spray of chemical mist, which is
noxious to breath in, but seemingly critical

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:45.770
for the growth of the columns. Light from
the drones’ glowing, bulbous shells cuts

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:52.370
through the gloom, bathing the chamber in
an eerie glow. Like dutiful worker bees, the

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:58.910
drones stay fixed on their role in this biomechanical
ecosystem, never reacting to anything that

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:08.490
passes by. In the epicenter of the greenhouse,
the tissue towers are overshadowed by a sinister

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:15.630
central tree. This massive organism is far
larger than anything else in the region, and

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:21.070
appears to be the parent of the unsettling
forest that grows around it. A unique type

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:26.980
of carrier drone tends to this goliath, taking
vials of the fertilizing chemical and plugging

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:37.190
them directly into the tree. Yet these chemicals
aren’t the only fuel source in The Assembly.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:42.790
Even deeper within the facility, a strange
structure with branching pods seems to supply

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:49.300
energy. Activating this device will cause
the pods to open in petal-like arrangements,

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:55.620
revealing humanoids kept in suspended animation
inside. These humanoids are closer to the

00:09:55.620 --> 00:10:00.630
player’s character than the Moldmen, and
call to mind the living batteries seen in

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:07.061
the dystopian future in films like The Matrix.
The more you explore the lower levels of The

00:10:07.061 --> 00:10:14.380
Assembly, the more the interiors become increasingly
organ-like. Extendable bridges link up sections

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:19.740
like valves in a heart. Tubes twist through
the region like veins — and some literally

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:27.269
seem to be veins. A corrosive substance analogous
to stomach acid clears up obstructions. Interacting

00:10:27.269 --> 00:10:33.269
with these systems requires the continued
use of technology. Scorn does include some

00:10:33.269 --> 00:10:38.890
traditional video game items like keys and
weapons, but filtered through a biomechanical

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:45.889
lens they become unrecognizable. A device
that opens doors looks like a fleshy worm.

00:10:45.889 --> 00:10:50.360
A key that you pick up elsewhere in the game
resembles ringed vertebrae — making it a

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:56.760
literal skeleton key. To reload your main
weapon, you approach this nightmarish machine,

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:03.460
which loads bug-like ammunition into a living
starfish-satchel. Thematically, these visually

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:10.720
disquieting interactions seem to examine the
relationship between humanity and technology.

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:15.540
Exploring the intersection of the organic
and inorganic is a theme that’s unsurprisingly

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:21.100
central to the biomechanical art movement.
Returning once again to Giger, while it’s

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:26.630
easy just to focus on the cool-looking surface
imagery, much of Giger’s work is meant to

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:32.041
interrogate whether humans are losing themselves
to technology. It’s a question that in the

00:11:32.041 --> 00:11:38.149
modern age only grows more and more urgent,
as opportunities to interface with,--and disappear

00:11:38.149 --> 00:11:45.410
into-- technology become increasingly prevalent.
Likewise, in Scorn, the playable character

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:51.709
themselves is changed over the course of the
game. At various points in the early chapters,

00:11:51.709 --> 00:11:58.170
the player can spot a strange creature scuttling
like a lizard along the walls. Called ‘The

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:02.880
Parasite,’ this lifeform eventually merges
with the protagonist, providing them with

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:09.320
useful abilities but slowly taking over their
body. To complicate this theme, the parasite

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:15.350
is themselves a warped humanoid who, after
having been exposed to mysterious chemicals,

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:20.399
has lost their identity, with various subtle
hints indicating they’re the humanoid the

00:12:20.399 --> 00:12:25.370
player takes control of during the game’s
prologue. While most of Scorn explores how

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:30.850
machinery can assimilate the organic, the
parasite storyline seems to explore autonomy

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:38.900
from a different perspective, asking where
one identity ends and another begins. Near

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:45.310
The Assembly lies a gaping abyss known as
The Crater. A forgotten void, the depths of

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:51.250
The Crater are home to far more dangerous
forms of life. Presiding over this nightmarish

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:57.589
domain is a gargantuan, nameless monarch.
Larger than a building, the creature acts

00:12:57.589 --> 00:13:03.320
as an insect queen to the life that infects
The Crater, constantly producing new forms

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:09.160
of workers. These smaller, more animalistic
terrors patrol the corridors surrounding The

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:15.480
Crater, and take on a variety of shambling
forms. According to game director Ljubomir

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:22.040
Peklar, these beings are made from the same
genetic material, but undergo rapid evolutionary

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:29.210
changes, resulting in distorted monstrosities
with occasionally recognizable animal features.

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:34.860
In some areas, flying creatures have evolved,
flitting about the corners of the player’s

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:40.430
vision, menacing you from a distance. All
these creatures serve the monarch at The Crater’s

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:46.190
center, and will rush to their defense if
they are harmed. And most every member of

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:53.079
the brood is dangerous, with the largest creatures
worth avoiding at all costs. Like snails leaving

00:13:53.079 --> 00:13:59.880
a trail of slime, the creatures of The Crater
leave behind twisting masses of red tendrils.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:04.880
These tentacles ungulate slowly, and seem
to be turning the atmosphere of The Crater

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:11.810
into something more comfortable for the brood.
In some places, this malignant infection has

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:18.180
become as dense as a rainforest, as if an
entirely new ecosystem is emerging in these

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:27.949
cavernous depths. In the distance, a vast
rail line promises a path away from this vile

00:14:27.949 --> 00:14:34.269
land. If you reach the station, the tracks
run for many miles. But eventually, the railway

00:14:34.269 --> 00:14:42.259
takes you to Scorn’s final destination — the
empty city of Polis. At a glance, Polis seems

00:14:42.259 --> 00:14:49.440
entirely unlike other regions, with clean
walls and elegant, sculptural architecture.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:55.339
Yet the more of this abandoned city the player
explores, the more you notice the darkness

00:14:55.339 --> 00:15:01.740
that dwells under the surface. Inside the
central building, impossibly ornate murals

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:08.790
depict unsettling scenes with reverent detail.
These twisted tableaus show humanoids being

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:14.620
turned into something else. The citizens of
Polis seemed to worship transcendence from

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:20.930
a corporeal form, with the destruction of
‘self’ an ever-present theme. In this

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:27.120
regard, Scorn evokes the work of Giger a final
time — as he was known for making similar

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:34.690
murals. In the epicenter of the room, a bronze
automaton beckons to the sky. Above the statue’s

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:40.199
head, the vaulted ceiling is covered in an
immense network of neuron-like strings of

00:15:40.199 --> 00:15:46.320
tissue. Given all we have seen, it appears
likely that the vanished citizens of Polis

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:51.970
live within this network, having abandoned
their physical bodies in favor of a mental

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:57.670
collective. What it is like to be part of
this collective is an open question, for those

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:05.160
within no longer have the means to answer.
Yet this city is not completely empty. Locked

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:12.050
away in jars are the pale, diminutive Homunculi,
artificial beings who appear to serve as living

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:19.050
batteries. Like many lifeforms in Scorn, these
creatures were created as a living resource,

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:25.149
their only individuality determined by the
shape of their jars. Yet Homunculi are quite

00:16:25.149 --> 00:16:31.000
intelligent, able to create and interface
with cybernetic bodies to overcome their physical

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:36.880
confines. While in-game you don’t encounter
these creatures much outside the rare boss

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:42.810
fight, concept art shows their resourcefulness,
depicting them assembling cyborgs like car

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:50.690
mechanics in a garage. Although not cited
as a direct influence, many aspects of Scorn’s

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:56.390
worldbuilding evoke the demonic realm depicted
in Barlowe’s Inferno. An artbook by the

00:16:56.390 --> 00:17:02.530
legendary Wayne Barlowe, Barlowe’s Inferno
envisions an infernal afterlife where all

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:09.370
the souls and bodies of inhabitants are a
resource used by a diabolical ruling class.

00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:14.970
While visually the two works are in many ways
distinct, the worlds possess intriguing thematic

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:21.079
parallels. Whether or not the nightmarish
realm of Scorn is a literal hell is largely

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:26.820
left up to interpretation. But if a land is
so bleak that it’s impossible to distinguish

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:32.570
from a plane of eternal torment, there’s
an argument to be made that, on some level,

00:17:32.570 --> 00:17:41.750
the distinction ceases to matter… But perhaps
there is salvation in the world of Scorn.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:47.351
In the back of the central building, behind
a far more biological-looking mural, a door

00:17:47.351 --> 00:17:53.410
opens up to a long walkway. At the end of
this path is a swirling matrix of energy that

00:17:53.410 --> 00:18:00.330
seems to offer a form of transcendence. Before
the threshold of the nexus, statues of humanoids

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:06.909
melt into dreamlike strands, indicating what
will happen if you step inside. Perhaps entering

00:18:06.909 --> 00:18:12.600
this matrix is a way to enter the collective
the citizens of Polis seem to have joined,

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:17.950
or perhaps it’s something else entirely.
In any case, it’s implied that converging

00:18:17.950 --> 00:18:24.320
with this nexus — whatever it truly is — would
mean complete annihilation, the total loss

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:31.130
of one’s self. Is this really salvation,
or just another form of destruction? I certainly

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:36.030
won’t give away the ending to Scorn in this
video, but suffice to say, the game never

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:41.909
turns its back on the exceedingly bleak atmosphere
it’s so carefully crafted throughout its

00:18:41.909 --> 00:18:50.380
storyline. The world that Scorn builds is
a grim, disturbing land of ceaseless torment.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:56.000
There’s a lot of unsettling imagery in this
game, much more than I’ve shown on screen

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:01.910
here, so be aware of that if you plan to check
it out for yourself. Yet the world of Scorn,

00:19:01.910 --> 00:19:08.340
for all its alien qualities, is undeniably
familiar. From the factories, to the barren

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:13.620
environments, to the droves of people losing
themselves to the bittersweet oblivion of

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:19.860
technology, the game is full of connections
to the current state of our species. And piecing

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:24.731
together subtle worldbuilding details and
interviews with the game’s creators, it

00:19:24.731 --> 00:19:30.820
seems that Scorn is indeed supposed to be
a future version of Earth, where the catastrophic

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:37.850
misuse of technology has left our species
unrecognizable. Ultimately, Scorn represents

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:44.380
a worst-case scenario for the story of humanity.
With all the potential this world has, I hope

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:52.100
the developers continue it in some form, and
show us more of their biomechanical vision...

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:58.700
As always, thanks for watching. If you enjoyed
this entry, please lend your support by liking,

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:04.750
subscribing, and hitting the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:13.909
See you in the next video.

